00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:10.400
just going to pull
that into the cluster,

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.020
and that's going
to start running.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.300
Now, here's the next cool
thing that you can do in GKE.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.820
We've done a lot of work to
make pods first class citizens

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:20.590
inside of Google Cloud.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.630
So you'll notice here
every pod in Kubernetes

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:23.932
gets its own IP address.

00:09:23.932 --> 00:09:25.390
Now, the one thing
that we do is we

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:29.720
make those IPs native using a
technology we call IP aliases.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.240
And what IP aliases
does, it takes

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.340
all of those container
IPs and makes

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.530
them first class in my VPC.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:39.430
So my development box is
a VM inside of my VPC.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.590
So what I should be
able to do is actually

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:44.740
without any port
forwarding or extra hops,

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:48.370
I should be to make a call to my
API service using the container

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:49.180
IP.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:50.170
And look at that--

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:51.880
directly talking
to the container

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.260
using a very natural
development flow, where

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.140
I can work on another
app while talking to

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.550
apps inside of the cluster.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:01.351
That's pretty dope, if
you didn't think so.

00:10:01.351 --> 00:10:02.345
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.570
All right, so we're
moving along here.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.736
The next thing we want to
do is get a web front end.

00:10:08.736 --> 00:10:11.730
Again, you kind of establish
some patterns here.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.160
Build a container, push it
up, and describe to Kubernetes

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.220
what you want to do.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.490
So here we'll build
our weather front end.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.000
And then we'll do
this deployment.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.760
Now, here's where things get
a little bit interesting.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:21.840
We're not going to run one copy.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.756
We're going to let
Kubernetes run three copies

00:10:23.756 --> 00:10:25.350
across that cluster for us.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.090
Now, once all of
this is running,

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.660
I have Envoy in a preconfigured
IP and a DNS address

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.120
that's pointing to this
particular back end.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.177
So what I'm going to
do now is pull this up.

00:10:36.177 --> 00:10:38.260
And we should be able to
hit the weather back end.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.320
Oh, things are looking good.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.300
Let's see.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.380
All right.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:44.940
Everything seems to be working.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:46.410
Now here's the thing--

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.720
I've been the sole
developer this whole time.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.820
I know the front end
talks to the API server.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:54.240
And the API server
talks to the database.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.520
But what happens is someone
else on your team needs

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:57.960
to check things out?

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.070
The nice thing is I've
been using OpenCensus.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:02.070
So again, I don't actually
have a service match.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.040
But you can adopt all these
technologies a la cart.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.380
So if you want to add
tracing to your application,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.050
you can bring in tools
like the OpenCensus library

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.390
that allows you to decorate
your function calls

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:15.660
or calls between apps and
send that data to tools

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.550
like Zipkin or Jaeger.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.260
In my case, I'm
using Google Cloud,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.480
so we're going to send
it's a Stackdriver Trace.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:25.277
I'm going to pop over here
to the tracing console.

00:11:25.277 --> 00:11:27.610
And then we're going to find
one of those recent traces.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.500
So we can see one that just
came in roughly around now.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.050
And we'll click on it.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.850
And you can actually
see the end to end.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.710
Traffic comes into the
front end, the API.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:39.650
And then the API returns but not
before talking to the database.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:40.990
Now, here's a nice thing--

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.390
if you trace this inside
of your code base,

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.870
and I click Show Events,
I can come down here

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.860
and see the exact
SQL query that's

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.246
being executed at
that particular hop.

00:11:50.246 --> 00:11:52.870
Now, that gives you the ability
to actually see what's going on

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.161
and give people a lot more
insight without checking out

00:11:55.161 --> 00:11:57.800
the code.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.530
So we got all the
patterns in place--

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.420
infrastructure, tracing.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.460
And now we need to think
about something new.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.020
Now, we only have
about five minutes

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:07.670
left so we're going to
have to simulate this.

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.949
This is-- when your boss
shows up and is like, hey,

00:12:09.949 --> 00:12:11.240
we need some new functionality.

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.210
Can you get it done
by Friday, right?

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.649
So the task is on.

00:12:15.649 --> 00:12:16.940
So we have this infrastructure.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:18.512
We got this nice pattern set up.

00:12:18.512 --> 00:12:19.970
And what we want
to do now is start

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.803
to think about something
cool we can add in.

00:12:21.803 --> 00:12:23.020
We've got a web front end.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.860
But that's like so 2017.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:28.530
You got to have a voice
integration with your app

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:29.750
if you want to be cool.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.760
If you can't talk
to your application,

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.020
you're probably doing it wrong.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.070
So what we want to do
is think about a tool

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:37.920
called Dialogue Flow.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.600
You're not going to build your
own natural language processing

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.270
system from scratch.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.170
Some of you are probably trying.

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:46.530
There's a better way.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.030
So we're going to
use Dialogue Flow.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.240
So again, let's walk to
the pattern really quickly.

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:52.870
We're going to write
the application.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.600
And then we're going to
build it and push it up.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.280
Now, remember, the
container image

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.902
is just the price of admission.

00:12:58.902 --> 00:12:59.860
So we'll build this up.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.235
Now, here's the
other thing-- you

00:13:01.235 --> 00:13:04.960
won't be the sole developer
working on this application

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:05.807
forever.

00:13:05.807 --> 00:13:07.390
Maybe there's someone
else on the team

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:08.770
that wants to contribute.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:12.370
And maybe they have no
interest in writing YAML files

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.890
or using Kubernetes
from the command line.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:18.160
We've done a lot of work to make
sure that Kubernetes works well

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:19.420
from the UI.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.950
So we'll come over here.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:22.690
We'll go to the
Kubernetes console.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.490
And we can click on Workloads.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:27.040
Now, notice here, all the
things I did on the command line

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.440
are reflected in
the GKE console.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.662
Now, if I have a new
person on the team that's

00:13:32.662 --> 00:13:34.120
not quite familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:35.430
this could be their workflow.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.420
We'll click Deploy.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:39.100
And we've done
something special here.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:42.140
We've integrated deeply with
GCP, including the Google

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.570
Container Registry.

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:45.910
So if I click on
this, I can drop down

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:48.510
to find that image
that I just created.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.610
It finds all the right tags.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.660
Hit Select, and
then it fills out

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.400
everything I need underneath.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.390
Let's give this a name.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:59.050
We'll call it the
Weather Assistant.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:00.214
We have the right labels.

00:14:00.214 --> 00:14:01.630
And if I have
multiple clusters, I

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:03.449
can choose where
I should deploy.

00:14:03.449 --> 00:14:05.740
Now, if you want to see what's
going on under the hood,

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.320
you can actually display
that sweet, sweet YAML

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.280
that you love so much.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:12.370
[LAUGHS] And then we also give
you an autoscaler to make sure

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.830
you can respond to
traffic changes.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:16.760
And we'll hit Deploy.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.930
So that's going to do all
the heavy lifting for me

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:19.960
right through the UI.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:22.210
And just like that,
it should be deployed.

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:23.750
We'll switch over
here, and we can

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:27.509
see the Weather Assistant is
now ready, serving traffic.

00:14:27.509 --> 00:14:29.050
Now, the next thing
we're going to do

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:31.940
is make sure that we're ready
by checking the health check.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.300
So here's the DNS name
that I've assigned

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.040
to this particular app.

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:36.940
We'll hit it.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.710
We see everything is working.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:41.530
And we can also see all my
track is flowing through Envoy.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:43.292
I should be in good shape.

00:14:43.292 --> 00:14:44.750
So we only have a
few minutes left.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:46.624
Let's see if we can pull
this last piece off.

00:14:46.624 --> 00:14:48.700
So I want to log into the
Dialogue Flow console.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.250
This is also part of the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:55.000
So Dialogue Flow allows you to
design these voice integrations

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.660
with your application.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:57.592
So we'll come here.

00:14:57.592 --> 00:14:59.300
There's a couple of
things we need to do.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:01.780
First, we need to make sure
that our users have a good time

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.020
with our application.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.436
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:15:04.436 --> 00:15:06.580
going to define all
this verified inputs

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:08.470
and create a few synonyms,
just in case they

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.150
want to say things like
Los Angeles or Los Angeles,

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:14.540
California, USA to talk
to the weather app.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:17.170
The next thing we're going to
do is describe their intent.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:19.190
What does our user want to do?

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:21.580
So here we want to have
them check the temperature.

00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:23.500
Now, there's no way
I'm going to define

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.440
everything or every
phrase our users would do.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:28.580
So here what I'm going to
do is give Dialogue Flow

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.370
a few training phrases.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.350
And notice how it's
automatically pulling out

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.010
those regions that match the
entities that I described

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:35.890
before.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:37.390
So we're ready to go.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:40.649
Now I want to send this our web
hub that we just got deployed.

00:15:40.649 --> 00:15:41.440
So we'll come here.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:42.970
We have a couple of more steps.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:44.620
We'll go to Fulfillment.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.870
And from Fulfillment
you'll see that I'm just

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.960
putting in that same URL that
I had earlier that's pointing

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:53.320
to my Kubernetes stack.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.480
You have all kind of
integrations that you can do.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:58.400
But this time we just want
to do the Google Assistant.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.090
Now, this whole time I've
been using my Pixelbook

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:03.290
to do this demo.

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:04.940
And this is something
I also think

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.260
a part of the end-to-end
experience at Google.

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:08.660
So we want to test out this.

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:11.660
Now, I could use the simulator,
or I can actually come down

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:14.270
here and use my laptop.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:15.959
Now, to prove that
this is real, we're

00:16:15.959 --> 00:16:17.750
going to look at the
logs for the assistant

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.540
to make sure Kelsey
isn't cheating.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:24.655
So kutectl logs, we'll give
it that container name.

00:16:24.655 --> 00:16:26.030
And we just stream
the logs here.

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.620
So we can see it's listening,
but there's nothing there yet.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.050
So if I hit the Assistant
button on my Pixelbook,

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:35.510
we should be able
to talk this thing.

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.730
Yeah, whatever.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.934
All right, talk to the
local Weather Service.

00:16:40.934 --> 00:16:42.100
WEATHER ASSISTANT APP: Sure.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:45.280
Here's the test version
